  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Monday)\nIssued at 1228 PM CDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nHot and humid conditions will continue to plague the Mid-South\nover the next seven days. Heat Advisories will remain in effect\nthrough at least Friday, and likely be extended through the\nweekend.\n\nMidday temperatures across the Mid-South are in the upper 80s to\nlow 90s under clear skies. A 594dam ridge is centered over south\nColorado and north Mexico with another ridge of similar strength\nover the central Gulf Coast. Over the next 36 hours, the ridge to\nour west will shift over the Plains, and the ridge to our south\nwill remain generally stationary. These features will essentially\ncombine to form a large ridge encompassing the Plains and\nmiddle/south Mississippi River valley over the next 48 to\n72 hours keeping the Mid-South hot and dry. Northwest Tennessee,\nmainly north of I-40, could see an isolated thunderstorm\ntomorrow, where northwest flow is slightly more prominent, but\nthat is highly contingent on the development of an upstream MCS\ntracking into the region. Most short-term guidance keeps any\nconvection to our north and east over central Kentucky and\neventually into Middle Tennessee. Instability across West\nTennessee will be high to extreme due to the high temperatures\nand dew points, featuring steep lapse rates over -7C/km and CAPE\nover 3500 j/kg. Weak shear closer to the northwest flow across\nthe Ohio Valley into the Appalachians may support strong to\nsevere storm clusters, but storms should struggle closer to the\nridge farther south and west.\n\nThe ridge will strengthen after midweek, and become more centered\nover the southern and  middle Mississippi River Valley. As a\nresult, temperatures should trend warmer and rain chances will\nremain minimal. Extreme Heat Warnings for Heat Indices over\n109 degrees may be needed.",
